  "documentation": {
    "range": "<p>This species is found in brackish water along the West African coast, from Senegal to Angola, and on the islands of the Gulf of Guinea.<br/><br/><strong>Central</strong> <strong>Africa</strong>: It is known from Boma, Banana, Bulabemba and Malela, Lower Congo River. It is also known from Vista and Moanda on the coast of the Democratic Republic of the Congo.&#160;<br/><br/><strong>Western Africa</strong>:&#160;This species is distributed from Senegal to Cameroon, occasionally out of freshwaters, but always close to the coast.</p>",
    "population": "<em>Periophthalmus barbarus&#160;</em>is&#160;common in museum collections and can be locally abundant. The overall population trend is unknown.<em></em>",
    "habitats": "<em>Periophthalmus barbarus</em>&#160;is found on muddy substrates and in brackish waters of estuaries, lagoons, and mangrove swamps (Stiassny&#160;<em>et al.&#160;</em>2007). It is found occasionally in freshwater, but always close to the coast (Stiassny<em>&#160;et al.</em>&#160;2007).<em> </em>It<em> </em>is an amphibious air-breather (Martin and Bridges 1999, Reide 2004) that skips or walks on sand or mud in search of food; that is, on exposed intertidal mud flats of estuarine mangrove swamps. The adults feed chiefly on arthropods (crabs, insects, etc.) of the mud surface. Also included in the diet is the white mangrove,&#160;<em>Avicennia nitida</em>&#160;(Irvine <em>et al. </em>1947). It spawns in burrows (Miller 1990).",
    "threats": "<em>Periophthalmus barbarus</em><em>&#160;</em>may be&#160;impacted&#160;by coastal  development, water pollution, and destructive fishing practices in parts  of its range because of its coastal nature. However, these threats  would be localised.",
    "measures": "No conservation actions are known to be in place for this species. Its distribution overlaps several marine protected areas. Policy-based actions are needed to protect this species (regulation of fishing quotas).",
    "use_trade": "While there is no commercial fishery for&#160;<em>Periophthalmus barbarus</em>, it<em>&#160;</em>is harvested for human consumption, bait, and the aquarium trade.",
    "rationale": "<em>Periophthalmus barbarus </em>is endemic to the west African coast from Senegal to Angola. It<em> </em>is&#160;common in museum collections and can be locally abundant. While there is no commercial fishery for&#160;<em>Periophthalmus barbarus</em>, it<em>&#160;</em>is harvested for human consumption, bait, and the aquarium trade.&#160;<em>Periophthalmus barbarus</em><em>&#160;</em>may be&#160;impacted&#160;by coastal development, water pollution, and destructive fishing practices in parts of its range because of its coastal nature. However, these threats would be localised. There are no known species-specific conservation measures for&#160;<em>Periophthalmus barbarus</em>, but its distribution overlaps several marine protected areas. Therefore,&#160;<em>Periophthalmus barbarus </em>is listed as&#160;Least Concern.",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": null
  },
